# Local secrets for `wrangler dev`. Copy to `.dev.vars` (gitignored).
#
# This worker deliberately holds no IMAP credential — the sync worker owns it,
# and writes reach that worker over a service binding (#12).
#
# There is nothing to put here for Cloudflare Access either. The gate reads
# `ctx.access`, which the runtime populates and no file can; the one value it
# compares against, ACCESS_AUD, is a `vars` entry in wrangler.jsonc rather than
# a secret. This file stays as the place a real secret would go.

# Worth knowing before you reach for `wrangler dev`:
#
# Access is not in front of a local worker, so `ctx.access` is undefined and
# every local request is answered 401. That is the gate failing closed, not a
# bug. To drive the tools locally as an authenticated caller, add a dev block to
# your own copy of wrangler.jsonc — it is read only by `wrangler dev`:
#
#   "access": { "dev": { "aud": "<the same value as vars.ACCESS_AUD>",
#                        "identity": { "email": "you@example.com" } } }
#
# Remove it to go back to simulating an unauthenticated request. Most work on
# the MCP tools happens through `pnpm run test` / `test:watch` instead, which
# drive the handler directly.
